window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.4","title":"z-Tests for Means and Proportions","goal":"Run a z-test for a mean (the 50 bolts) and a z-test for a proportion (184 of 200 customers satisfied), by hand and with statsmodels.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"A z-test for a mean, by hand","text":"A line makes 10 mm bolts with a known SD of 0.5 mm. A sample of 50 bolts averages 10.14 mm. The z statistic is the gap divided by the standard error, and the two-sided p-value comes from the normal curve.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nmu0, sigma, n, xbar = 10, 0.5, 50, 10.14\n\nse = sigma / np.sqrt(n)\nz = (xbar - mu0) / se\np = 2 * stats.norm.sf(abs(z))\nci = (xbar - 1.96 * se, xbar + 1.96 * se)\n\nprint(f'z = {z:.3f}, p = {p:.4f}')\nprint(f'95% CI: ({ci[0]:.3f}, {ci[1]:.3f})')","out":"z = 1.980, p = 0.0477\n95% CI: (10.001, 10.279)","figs":[]},{"title":"The same test with ztest()","text":"statsmodels needs raw data. We build 50 pretend bolt readings with mean 10.14 and SD 0.5, so the result matches the hand calculation. Note that ztest() estimates the SD from the data rather than using a known sigma.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.weightstats import ztest\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nx = rng.normal(size=50)\nbolts = 10.14 + 0.5 * (x - x.mean()) / x.std(ddof=1)   # mean 10.14, SD 0.5\n\nz_stat, p_val = ztest(bolts, value=10, alternative='two-sided')\nprint(f'mean = {bolts.mean():.2f}, sd = {bolts.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f'z = {z_stat:.3f}, p = {p_val:.4f}')","out":"mean = 10.14, sd = 0.50\nz = 1.980, p = 0.0477","figs":[]},{"title":"A z-test for a proportion","text":"A company claims 95% satisfaction, but 184 of 200 customers are satisfied. The claim says 'at least 95%', so we test the left tail ('smaller'). We set prop_var=0.95 so the standard error uses the claimed value, as a test should.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportions_ztest\n\nz_prop, p_prop = proportions_ztest(count=184, nobs=200, value=0.95,\n                                   alternative='smaller', prop_var=0.95)\n\nprint(f'sample proportion = {184 / 200:.2f}')\nprint(f'z = {z_prop:.3f}')\nprint(f'left-tailed p = {p_prop:.4f}')\nprint('reject H0 at 0.05?', p_prop <= 0.05)","out":"sample proportion = 0.92\nz = -1.947\nleft-tailed p = 0.0258\nreject H0 at 0.05? True","figs":[]},{"title":"Why the standard error matters","text":"If we leave out prop_var, statsmodels uses the observed 0.92 in the standard error. This is the classic mistake from the lesson, and it changes z and p.","code":"z_wrong, p_wrong = proportions_ztest(count=184, nobs=200, value=0.95,\n                                     alternative='smaller')\n\nprint(f'with the observed proportion: z = {z_wrong:.3f}, p = {p_wrong:.4f}')\nprint(f'with the claimed 0.95:         z = {z_prop:.3f}, p = {p_prop:.4f}')","out":"with the observed proportion: z = -1.564, p = 0.0589\nwith the claimed 0.95:         z = -1.947, p = 0.0258","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"# z-test for a mean (sigma known), by hand: base R has no z.test\nz <- (10.14 - 10) / (0.5 / sqrt(50))\nz\n2 * pnorm(-abs(z))\n\n# z-test for a proportion, no continuity correction\nres <- prop.test(184, 200, p = 0.95, alternative = \"less\", correct = FALSE)\nres\n-sqrt(res$statistic)   # the z value (z squared is X-squared)","expect":"z = 1.9799, two-sided p = 0.04771; X-squared = 3.7895, df = 1, p-value = 0.02579 (z = -1.9467)","note":"prop.test reports X-squared, which is z squared. For a one-sided test its p-value equals the z-test p-value, and correct = FALSE switches off the continuity correction so it matches."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (SPSS has no z-test menu; use the normal-curve functions)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / xbar mu0 sigma n x m p0.\nBEGIN DATA\n10.14 10 0.5 50 184 200 0.95\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE z_mean = (xbar - mu0) / (sigma / SQRT(n)).\nCOMPUTE p_mean = 2 * (1 - CDF.NORMAL(ABS(z_mean), 0, 1)).\nCOMPUTE z_prop = (x / m - p0) / SQRT(p0 * (1 - p0) / m).\nCOMPUTE p_prop = CDF.NORMAL(z_prop, 0, 1).\nLIST z_mean p_mean z_prop p_prop.","note":"The first test uses the known sigma; the second is the left-tailed proportion test using p0 in the standard error. LIST shows 2 decimals by default; widen the columns in the output table to see more."},"stata":{"code":"display (10.14 - 10) / (0.5 / sqrt(50))\ndisplay 2 * (1 - normal(1.9799))\nprtesti 200 184 0.95, count","note":"Stata has no one-line z-test for a mean from summary numbers in older versions, so the first two lines work by hand. prtesti is the one-proportion z-test (it uses 0.95 in the standard error); read the Pr(Z < z) line for the left-tailed p-value."},"try":["Change the sample mean to 10.05 and predict whether the p-value gets bigger or smaller, then run it.","Change the alternative from 'smaller' to 'two-sided' in the proportion test and predict what happens to p."]});
